Choose distinct <prime numbers> $p,q$ so large that $pq>C(p+q)$, and set
$$
\alpha=\frac1p,
\qquad
\beta=\frac1q.
$$
The fraction $(p+q)/(pq)$ is reduced, because neither $p$ nor $q$ divides $p+q$. The <height of a rational number> therefore gives
$$
H(\alpha+\beta)=pq,
\qquad
H(\alpha)+H(\beta)=p+q.
$$
The choice of $p,q$ proves the required strict inequality.

